I entirely disagree with KPD. Adding extra #ifdefs to turn on or off
features just makes a mess of the code, and the mess tends to linger
long after the patched version has become standard. Furthermore,
#ifdefs multiply, and there is no easy way to get rid of them once
they're in there. Nethack has #ifdef'ed code that dates back years and
years. I doubt anyone have tested that it even compiles properly with
some of those flags turned off (Hands up who #undefines TOUIRST.
Anyone?) These are a real pain when it comes to writing new patches.

If you don't want a patch, don't install it.
If you want a feature to be optional, make it an option.
If you really _must_ have #ifdefs, learn to use the -D option on your
'patch' program.

Malcolm

